        <section>
          <h2>From Teacher to Missionary</h2>

          <p>
            Mother Teresa was born Anjezë Gonxhe Bojaxhiu in Skopje on
            August 26, 1910. At eighteen, she left home to join the Sisters of
            Loreto and soon traveled to India. She arrived in Calcutta in
            January 1929 and eventually became a teacher and then principal at
            St. Mary’s school.
          </p>

          <p>
            Her life changed dramatically after September 10, 1946, when,
            during a train journey to Darjeeling, she experienced what she
            later called her “call within a call”—a conviction that she was
            being called to leave the convent and live among and serve “the
            poorest of the poor.”
          </p>

          <p>
            Before beginning that work, Mother Teresa undertook a short period
            of medical training with the Medical Missionaries in Patna in 1948.
            She then returned to Calcutta, where she visited the sick, taught
            street children, and established a school and dispensary in the
            Motijhil slums.
          </p>

          <p>
            On October 7, 1950, the Missionaries of Charity was officially
            established. Medical care quickly became an important part of its
            work. The Missionaries of Charity’s own history records the
            establishment of medical dispensaries, a home for the dying in
            1952, a home for abandoned children in 1955, and a mobile leprosy
            clinic in 1957.
          </p>

          <p class="source-link">
            <a
              href="https://missionariesofcharity.org/about-us/mother-teresa/"
              target="_blank"
              rel="noopener noreferrer"
            >
              Missionaries of Charity — Mother Teresa, Our Foundress →
            </a>
          </p>
        </section>

        <section>
          <h2>A Homeopathic Dispensary in Calcutta</h2>

          <p>
            One of the most intriguing accounts comes from Dr. Valerian Thomas
            D’Souza, a career homeopathic physician who later served for
            decades as Chief Homoeopathic Physician at Father Muller’s Hospital
            in Mangalore.
          </p>

          <p>
            Writing about his early life, D’Souza recalled arriving in
            Calcutta on July 17, 1955 and meeting Mother Teresa shortly
            thereafter.
          </p>

          <p>
            According to his firsthand recollection, Mother Teresa permitted
            him to work in her:
          </p>

          <blockquote>
            “Homeopathic Free Dispensary at Kidderpore.”
          </blockquote>

          <p>
            D’Souza recalled working there under Dr. (Major) Nicholas, whom he
            described as having practiced conventional medicine while serving
            in the Army before becoming an eminent homeopath.
          </p>

          <p>
            D’Souza also remembered encountering Mother Teresa again later.
            According to his account, she remembered his previous work in the
            dispensary and encouraged him to return to Calcutta after
            Dr. Nicholas had gone to the United Kingdom.
          </p>

          <p>
            This is significant because D’Souza’s account does not originate
            with the later writers who popularized the story of Mother Teresa
            and homeopathy. It represents the retrospective testimony of a
            physician who claimed to have personally worked in the dispensary
            in 1955.
          </p>

          <p>
            As with any recollection published decades after an event, it
            should be identified as such. I have not yet located a surviving
            1955 dispensary record naming D’Souza or Nicholas. Nevertheless,
            D’Souza’s professional identity and career can be independently
            established, making his testimony an important piece of the
            historical record.
          </p>

          <p class="source-link">
            <a
              href="https://www.sezariworld.com/images/pdf/sec25-48all.pdf"
              target="_blank"
              rel="noopener noreferrer"
            >
              Read Dr. V. T. D’Souza’s published recollection →
            </a>
          </p>
        </section>

        <section>
          <h2>A Documented Gift to a Homeopathic Hospital</h2>

          <p>
            Another connection comes from an entirely different source.
          </p>

          <p>
            The institutional history of Mahesh Bhattacharyya Homoeopathic
            Medical College &amp; Hospital in Howrah records Mother Teresa’s
            support for the hospital during its development.
          </p>

          <p>The institution states:</p>

          <blockquote>
            “Mother Teresa was gracious enough to donate 25 beds with mattress
            through Mother Amie.”
          </blockquote>

          <p>
            The college had begun in 1967 and subsequently developed a
            charitable outpatient department, hospital facilities, and formal
            education in homeopathic medicine. Its history records Mother
            Teresa’s donation in the context of the hospital’s expansion.
          </p>

          <p>
            Unlike some of the broader stories surrounding Mother Teresa and
            homeopathy, this claim comes directly from the recipient
            homeopathic institution’s own historical record.
          </p>

          <p class="source-link">
            <a
              href="https://mbhmch.org/about%20us.html"
              target="_blank"
              rel="noopener noreferrer"
            >
              Mahesh Bhattacharyya Homoeopathic Medical College &amp; Hospital
              — Institutional History →
            </a>
          </p>
        </section>

        <section>
          <h2>Did Mother Teresa Personally Practice Homeopathy?</h2>

          <p>
            This is where the historical record becomes less certain.
          </p>

          <p>
            Homeopathy author Dana Ullman has reported a substantially deeper
            relationship between Mother Teresa, the Missionaries of Charity,
            and homeopathy. His account describes multiple charitable
            homeopathic dispensaries, Sisters studying homeopathy, and Mother
            Teresa herself sometimes prescribing remedies.
          </p>

          <p>
            Importantly, Ullman identifies individuals from whom he obtained
            information, specifically thanking Dr. Parameswar Bose and Dr. Iva
            Pal. Other versions of this historical account also identify
            Dr. (Sister) M. Gomes as a reference.
          </p>

          <p class="source-link">
            <a
              href="https://homeopathic.com/mother-teresa-and-homeopathy/"
              target="_blank"
              rel="noopener noreferrer"
            >
              Dana Ullman — Mother Teresa and Homeopathy →
            </a>
          </p>

          <p>
            These reports deserve to be preserved as part of the historical
            trail. However, during my research I have not yet located the
            original correspondence, interviews, Missionaries of Charity
            records, or other primary documentation necessary to independently
            verify these particular claims.
          </p>

          <p>
            That distinction is important.
          </p>

          <p>
            It also does not mean the accounts are untrue. Historical records
            disappear, private correspondence remains inaccessible, memories
            are never written down, and many acts of ordinary charitable work
            leave little documentary trace. Ullman’s named sources may have
            possessed direct knowledge unavailable in public archives.
          </p>

          <p>
            For that reason, I believe the most responsible approach is
            neither to present every reported detail as established fact nor to
            dismiss the accounts simply because I have not independently
            corroborated them.
          </p>

          <p>
            They remain reported historical evidence worthy of further
            investigation.
          </p>
        </section>

        <section>
          <h2>What About Dr. Diwan Jai Chand?</h2>

          <p>
            Another frequently repeated story states that Mother Teresa learned
            homeopathy from the prominent Indian homeopathic physician
            Dr. Diwan Jai Chand (1887–1961) and encouraged Sisters to study it
            as well.
          </p>

          <p>
            Jai Chand himself is not difficult to document. He was an important
            figure in twentieth-century Indian homeopathy, associated with
            homeopathic education, professional organizations, charitable
            medicine, and the journal <em>Health and Homoeopathy</em>.
          </p>

          <p>
            What I have not found is a primary or contemporary source
            establishing that Mother Teresa personally studied with him.
          </p>

          <p>
            I contacted the Central Council for Research in Homoeopathy (CCRH)
            in India while researching this question. Their library was unable
            to locate the material needed to establish the connection.
          </p>

          <p>
            Therefore, at present I regard the Mother Teresa–Jai Chand
            relationship as an interesting but unverified historical claim,
            rather than an established fact.
          </p>
        </section>

        <section>
          <h2>What the Evidence Allows Us to Say</h2>

          <p>
            After following these different historical trails, I believe
            several distinctions are important.
          </p>

          <p>
            Mother Teresa’s commitment to caring for the sick is exceptionally
            well documented. Her official biography records medical training
            in Patna before beginning her mission, and the Missionaries of
            Charity themselves document the establishment of dispensaries and
            other forms of medical care during the organization’s early years.
          </p>

          <p>
            I also have Dr. V. T. D’Souza’s firsthand retrospective testimony
            that Mother Teresa permitted him to work in her Homeopathic Free
            Dispensary at Kidderpore in 1955.
          </p>

          <p>
            Separately, a homeopathic medical college and hospital records
            Mother Teresa’s donation of 25 beds and mattresses to its hospital.
          </p>

          <p>
            Beyond those points lies a larger collection of accounts
            describing a much deeper involvement with homeopathy. Those
            accounts may ultimately prove accurate, but some of their
            underlying documentation remains elusive.
          </p>
        </section>

        <section>
          <h2>History Is a Continual Search</h2>

          <p>
            One of the things I have come to appreciate while researching
            Homeopathy in History is that historical research rarely provides
            every answer at once.
          </p>

          <p>
            Sometimes we find the original handwritten letter. Sometimes an
            institutional record preserves an unexpected detail. Sometimes a
            physician leaves behind a firsthand recollection decades later.
            And sometimes a story survives while the documents that could
            confirm it remain undiscovered.
          </p>

          <p>
            Mother Teresa’s relationship with homeopathy appears to contain a
            little of each.
          </p>

          <p>
            I will continue searching for letters, photographs, dispensary
            records, contemporary publications, and firsthand accounts that
            might help illuminate this fascinating chapter in the history of
            homeopathy.
          </p>

          <p>
            What I can document already tells an intriguing story: a woman
            whose life’s work centered on bringing compassionate care to people
            who had very little, and historical evidence placing homeopathy
            within at least part of that work.
          </p>
        </section>

